<div class="page">
<header class="masthead">
<p class="eyebrow">Fortress revival &middot; coordinator skill &middot; skill drafts &middot; version 20 &middot; 7 November 2026</p>
<h1>Your held rules, version 20</h1>
<p class="standfirst">Version 20. Two landed changes that you have not read. Item 28: your standing order on branches. Nobody tries to delete a branch on GitHub from this container, and the coordinator never asks or reminds you to delete one, since you delete them when you get to them. Item 29: after a light boot, the coordinator reads FACTS and POSITIONS whole at the first batch landing, as you asked this morning, now in plainer words at your comment of 06:19 UTC. Nothing waits for your yes.</p>
</header>

<nav class="parts" aria-label="Sections">
<h2>Sections</h2>
<ul>
<li><a href="#s1">Landed since your last approval</a></li>
<li><a href="#s2">Proposed changes</a></li>
<li><a href="#words">Words used on this page</a></li>
</ul>
</nav>

<section id="s1" class="part">
<h2>Landed since your last approval</h2>
<p>You read item 27 at 11:41 UTC on 6 November 2026, so it is not on the page.</p>
<p>Two items, 28 and 29, changes to the coordinator skill that you have not read. Each shows its change against the text before it.</p>
<p>In the item, the words that changed are underlined, and the words that went are struck through. To reverse a change, send a comment to Claude on the item, or say so in chat. The skill writer then restores the old words.</p>

<section id="l28" class="part item">
<h3><span class="num">Change 28 &middot; SKILL.md, &ldquo;Quiet times&rdquo; and &ldquo;The record: what goes where&rdquo;</span>Your standing order on branches</h3>
<div class="change solo"><div><span class="side">New text; underlined, the words that came in. No words went. In the skill's words, &ldquo;you&rdquo; is the coordinator.</span>
<p class="where-in">SKILL.md, &ldquo;Talking to the curator&rdquo;, &ldquo;Quiet times&rdquo;, a new bullet, line 186</p>
<ul><li><span class="add">The curator deletes the branches on GitHub, when they get to them. The permission check also refuses the deletion from this session. Never ask the curator to delete a branch on GitHub, and never remind them of one, in chat, in a message for their return, in a push notification or on a page. Drop a branch&rsquo;s deletion from every ask, close and list that the record holds. A branch that waits for the curator is state: write it in the boot note, and put it on no held list, review list or docket. If the curator asks about branches, answer from the tree.</span></li></ul>
<p class="where-in">SKILL.md, &ldquo;The record: what goes where&rdquo;, the bullet on pushing, line 235, one sentence added</p>
<ul><li>Push after every commit, in the order of <code>fortress-repo</code>&rsquo;s committing part. <span class="add">Do not try to delete a branch on GitHub: the permission check refuses it, and the curator deletes it.</span></li></ul>
</div></div>
<div class="note"><p><strong>Why.</strong> The skill had no rule on branches. The session&rsquo;s permission check refuses every deletion of a branch on GitHub from this container. Even so, the coordinator asked you twice to delete the scratch branch <code>tmp-push-probe</code>, the second time just before 12:00 UTC on 6 November. You answered that this was to have been a standing order: nobody tries such a deletion, and nobody asks or reminds you, since you delete the branches on GitHub when you get to them. The text now says so. It also drops a branch&rsquo;s deletion from every ask and list in the record, keeps a branch that waits for you as state in the boot note only, and has the coordinator answer from the tree if you ask about branches.</p>
<p>The cold read of the first text found that it also forbade deleting branches here in the container. The record-landing workflow makes such local deletions, and they work. The fixed text covers only the branches on GitHub.</p></div>
</section>

<section id="l29" class="part item">
<h3><span class="num">Change 29 &middot; SKILL.md, &ldquo;The boot&rdquo;, &ldquo;A light boot&rdquo; and &ldquo;At a landing&rdquo;</span>After a light boot, the first batch landing reads FACTS and POSITIONS whole</h3>
<div class="change solo"><div><span class="side">New text, against the text before the change; struck through, the words that went; underlined, the words that came in. In the skill's words, &ldquo;you&rdquo; is the coordinator.</span>
<p class="where-in">SKILL.md, &ldquo;The boot&rdquo;, &ldquo;A light boot&rdquo;, line 251, and its list of three steps, lines 253 to 255</p>
<p><strong>A light boot.</strong> When the curator asks for a light boot, take steps 0 and 3 to 8, and skip steps 1 and 2: FACTS, POSITIONS and INDEX stay unread. Before you state a fact or a decision, or put anything to the curator, search the record with <code>facts-extract.sh</code>. The full boot<span class="add">, steps 0 to 8,</span> is the default. The curator's word for a light boot replaces the hook's list of files after a compaction. <span class="add">After a light boot, do not read INDEX whole. Search it with <code>facts-extract.sh 'index:WORDS'</code>. Then take these steps:</span></p>
<ol>
<li><span class="add">At the first batch landing after the boot, read <code>FACTS.md</code> and <code>POSITIONS.md</code> whole, <code>FACTS.md</code> in pages. Do it before step 1 of <code>references/batch.md</code>, &ldquo;The landing&rdquo;. If the light boot falls inside a batch landing, after step 1 of &ldquo;The landing&rdquo;, read them whole at once, before its next step. Naming the decisions that landed and planning the batch after it need both files whole.</span></li>
<li><span class="add">When the record-landing workflow of step 4 of &ldquo;The landing&rdquo; ends, read what it changed in the two files.</span></li>
<li><span class="add">At a later batch landing in the same session, with no compaction between, read only what the batch landing changed, as after a full boot.</span></li>
</ol>
<p class="where-in">SKILL.md, &ldquo;The boot&rdquo;, &ldquo;At a landing&rdquo;, line 263</p>
<p><strong>At a landing.</strong> A <span class="add">batch</span> landing's work never waits for a compaction or a boot, and you ask the curator nothing about the boot. <span class="cut">At a landing,</span> <span class="add">After a full boot,</span> read what the <span class="add">batch</span> landing changed in the record, not FACTS and POSITIONS whole. <span class="cut">Read the whole record when you plan a batch.</span> <span class="add">After a light boot, follow the list under &ldquo;A light boot&rdquo;.</span></p>
<p class="where-in">references/sources.md, a new section, line 388, its heading and its lines</p>
<p><span class="add"><strong>&ldquo;Landing should trigger full load&rdquo;: the first batch landing after a light boot, 2026-11-07</strong></span></p>
<ul><li><span class="add"><code>SKILL.md</code>, &ldquo;The boot&rdquo;, &ldquo;A light boot&rdquo; and &ldquo;At a landing&rdquo;: after a light boot, the first batch landing after that boot starts a read of <code>FACTS.md</code> and <code>POSITIONS.md</code> whole; INDEX stays searched; the landing bullet&rsquo;s partial read holds after a full boot. The curator said &ldquo;landing&rdquo;; the skill reads only the first one whole, since a context that has read the two files whole holds them, so a later landing in it reads what the landing changed, as after a full boot. Settled by the coordinator. The curator&rsquo;s words in chat at 05:5x UTC on 2026-11-07: &ldquo;We should just add instruction to coordinator skill that landing should trigger full load of POSITIONS and FACTS in coordinator in light boot mode.&rdquo; His reason in the same message: a long chat in a cold context had cost much of the week&rsquo;s budget, so he boots light for the climb, and the landing&rsquo;s record work and the planning of the batch after it need the whole record.</span></li><li><span class="add">The cold read of <code>70900ce67</code>, 2026-11-07, read as the coordinating session; settled by the coordinator: the read placed before step 1 of <code>references/batch.md</code>, &ldquo;The landing&rdquo;, at the first batch landing after that boot; the reason tied to the whole read, as step 2&rsquo;s naming of decisions and review list and the planning of the batch after it; the paging a sentence of its own; the record-landing workflow&rsquo;s changes read after step 4; INDEX searched with <code>facts-extract.sh 'index:WORDS'</code>; &ldquo;At a landing&rdquo; brought in line, a later landing in the same context reading only the changes; the planning served by the landing&rsquo;s whole read; &ldquo;the full boot&rdquo; named as steps 0 to 8.</span></li><li><span class="add">The two passages rewritten in ASD-STE100&rsquo;s way, the rule unchanged: a numbered list under &ldquo;A light boot&rdquo;, and two sentences under &ldquo;At a landing&rdquo; that point to it; &ldquo;Read the whole record when you plan a batch&rdquo; cut: after a full boot the session holds the record from its boot and reads each landing&rsquo;s changes, and after a light boot INDEX is only searched. The curator&rsquo;s comment of 06:19 UTC on 2026-11-07 on the page of skill drafts: &ldquo;This reads like a clumsy tangle of spaghetti code. You can do better. ASD-STE100 is your guiding light.&rdquo;</span></li><li><span class="add">The cold read of <code>3afa70cfd</code>, 2026-11-07, read as the coordinating session; settled by the coordinator: INDEX&rsquo;s search a rule before the list, not a step; &ldquo;need both files whole&rdquo;; a light boot inside a batch landing reads the two files at once; &ldquo;step 1 of &rdquo;The landing&ldquo;&rdquo; against the boot&rsquo;s step 1; &ldquo;batch landing&rdquo; for &ldquo;landing&rdquo;, and &ldquo;in the same session, with no compaction between&rdquo; for &ldquo;in the same context&rdquo;.</span></li></ul>
</div></div>
<div class="note"><p><strong>Why.</strong> After a light boot, FACTS and POSITIONS stayed unread, and at a landing the coordinator read only what the landing changed. So a batch landing, where the coordinator names the decisions that landed and plans the batch after it, rested on searches of the record alone. You asked, in chat just before 06:00 UTC on 7 November, that a landing after a light boot read FACTS and POSITIONS whole.</p>
<p>The text now says this. The first batch landing after a light boot reads both files whole, before step 1 of &ldquo;The landing&rdquo;. After that, the coordinator reads what the record-landing workflow changed in them. A later batch landing in the same session reads only what it changed. INDEX is searched, never read whole. &ldquo;At a landing&rdquo; now points to the list of three steps in &ldquo;A light boot&rdquo;.</p>
<p>The skill writer rewrote the two passages at your comment of 06:19 UTC, in plainer words, with the rule unchanged.</p></div>
</section>

<div class="sources"><span class="label">References</span>
<ul>
<li>Item 28: commits of 6 November, <code>68efa8f06</code>, 12:32 UTC, &ldquo;coordinator, fortress-repo: the curator&rsquo;s standing order on branches&rdquo;, and <code>730daf06a</code>, 12:40 UTC, &ldquo;coordinator, fortress-repo: the cold read&rsquo;s fixes on the branch order&rdquo;. Shown as the net change of the two to the coordinator skill against the text before <code>68efa8f06</code>. The same commits also changed <code>fortress-repo</code>&rsquo;s committing part, which this page does not show.</li>
<li>Item 29: commits of 7 November, <code>70900ce67</code>, 05:57 UTC, &ldquo;coordinator: after a light boot, a landing reads FACTS and POSITIONS whole&rdquo;, <code>34b39a287</code>, 06:01 UTC, &ldquo;coordinator: the cold read&rsquo;s fixes on the landing rule&rdquo;, <code>3afa70cfd</code>, 06:21 UTC, &ldquo;coordinator: the landing rule said plainly&rdquo;, and <code>08c60aea8</code>, 06:24 UTC, &ldquo;coordinator: the cold read&rsquo;s fixes on the plain landing rule&rdquo;. Shown as the net change of the four against the text before <code>70900ce67</code>. Your words in chat at 05:5x UTC on 7 November: &ldquo;We should just add instruction to coordinator skill that landing should trigger full load of POSITIONS and FACTS in coordinator in light boot mode.&rdquo; Your comment of 06:19 UTC on 7 November on this item: &ldquo;This reads like a clumsy tangle of spaghetti code. You can do better. ASD-STE100 is your guiding light.&rdquo;</li>
