<dt>read mark</dt><dd>Your word that you read the page up to an item, such as a plain &ldquo;LGTM&rdquo; on a landed item, which drops that item and every older one at the next update.</dd>
<dt>boot note</dt><dd>File of what is in flight now, which the coordinator reads at each boot, with the links of the pages.</dd>
<dt>draft</dt><dd>Item under “Proposed changes” that waits for your yes.</dd>
<dt>page stage</dt><dd>Stage of the record-landing workflow that updates the page of <code>fortress-repo</code> changes.</dd>
<dt>diff</dt><dd>Text that shows what a change cut and added.</dd>
<dt>cold read</dt><dd>Reading of a changed part by a worker given only the skill, which marks each passage that would send it wrong or make it search.</dd>
<dt>authorship map</dt><dd>Map of who wrote what in the team's texts, compiled from the git history, in <code>research/authorship.md</code>.</dd>
<dt>cold reader</dt><dd>Worker that does a cold read.</dd>
<dt>flag</dt><dd>Passage that a cold read marks, with its problem and a proposed fix.</dd>
<dt>lint</dt><dd>Script that searches the skills' text for internal handles, status words and stale wording.</dd>
<dt>register</dt><dd>Way in which the coordinator writes to you, which the bullets at the head of “Talking to the curator” set.</dd>
<dt><code>fortress-repo</code></dt><dd>Skill that holds the rules that bind the workers in the repository.</dd>
<dt>revival part</dt><dd>Part of <code>fortress-repo</code> that tells workers how revived Fortress differs from the original.</dd>
<dt>closing docket</dt><dd>List of 3 November of the open items of the post-mortems and the held list, each with a proposed close.</dd>
<dt>commit stage</dt><dd>Last stage of a batch, which commits the landed tree and pushes it.</dd>
<dt>workflow journal</dt><dd>Log of a Workflow run, with a line for each agent's start and result.</dd>
<dt><code>spend.py</code></dt><dd>Script that counts the tokens written in the transcripts that changed after a given time.</dd>
<dt>quiz</dt><dd>Small set of decisions that a fresh worker answers from new entries, to test that their meaning stayed.</dd>
<dt>pilot</dt><dd>First run on three or four entries, which you read before the rest run.</dd>
<dt>chunk</dt><dd>Group of items that one writer takes in a round: about ten entries or rows in a long writing pass, 20 to 32 entries in a consolidation.</dd>
<dt>Workflow run</dt><dd>One launch of a workflow script by the Workflow tool, with its own ID and its own agents.</dd>
<dt>writer stream</dt><dd>Line of writer sessions in one run, each resumed while its context is small and its prompt cache warm.</dd>
<dt>judge</dt><dd>Agent at the end of a round that rules apply or hold on each point that a chunk's checks raise.</dd>
<dt>long writing pass</dt><dd>Run of a workflow that rewrites many entries or rows of the record, chunk by chunk.</dd>
<dt>record-landing workflow</dt><dd>Workflow that brings the record in line with a batch's landing.</dd>
<dt>revival page</dt><dd>Page that tells where the revival stands, which the record-landing workflow updates after a landing.</dd>
<dt>mining workflow</dt><dd>Workflow that finds where each POSITIONS entry came from.</dd>
<dt>clarity workflow</dt><dd>Workflow that rewrites entries so that each reads on its own.</dd>
<dt>orientation workflow</dt><dd>Workflow that writes and updates the revival part.</dd>
</dl>
